<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cirebon Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1430 CE – 1677 CE (approx.) · capital Cirebon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim kingdom on the north coast of Java, founded with Sunan Gunung Jati, one of the Nine Saints who spread Islam on the island.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> grew as a port on the border between Javanese and Sundanese lands. Tradition says Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walangsungsang">Walangsungsang</a>, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> king, founded a settlement there around 1430 and embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Syarif Hidayatullah</a>, known as Sunan Gunung Jati, ruled from the late 15th century and broke away from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak</a> and helped take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon's</a> courts blended Javanese, Sundanese, Chinese and Islamic culture, visible in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kasepuhan-sultanate">Kasepuhan</a> palace and its batik with cloud motifs. The port traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and textiles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> fell under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate</a>. In 1677, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten's</a> help, the realm was split between princes into the courts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kasepuhan-sultanate">Kasepuhan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kanoman-sultanate">Kanoman</a>, which soon accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> protection. The palaces and their sultans survive as cultural institutions today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cirebon-sultanate.jpg" alt="Cirebon Sultanate" width="320" height="177"><figcaption>Image: Fazoffic · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_1520s_Cirebon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1430 CE – 1677 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cirebon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walangsungsang, Sunan Gunung Jati, Panembahan Ratu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese (Cirebon), Sundanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Cirebon, Cheribon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=cirebon-sultanate" aria-label="Cirebon Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7685 2430 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cirebon Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8005 2686L8052 2725M8005 2686L8005 2686M8005 2686L8005 2686"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0M8005 2686h0M8005 2686h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8005 2686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8005 2686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cirebon. <a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=cirebon-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
